MTF Unit:  Iota-27 (“Art Connoisseurs”)

MTF Squad:  Delta-2

SCP-5487-1:  Waterfall by M. C. Escher

Task:  Discover any deviations from baseline reality.

 

(Lt. Gibson Sgt. Grant Pvt. Jones and Pvt. Williams enter SCP-5487-1 on the pathway at the bottom center of the painting. Pvt. Jones attempts to step to the left, but instead walks backwards. A 90 degree turn changes the direction of movement the equivalent of 180 degrees.)

Pvt. Jones:  What the [Data Explunged]

Lt. Gibson:  Let’s orient ourselves before we begin the investigation.

(The team moves around until they begin to comprehend how to love within SCP-5487-1.)

Lt. Gibson:  Ok. I think that’s enough. Sgt. Grant, why don’t you look for a way up there.

(Gibson points to the balcony above the archway to the left of where they are standing. Grant walks into the archway to the left of where they stand.)

Lt. Gibson:  Pvt. Jones and Pvt. Williams, why don’t you go down those stairs to our right. Report back in 10 minutes.

(Both privates walk away towards the staircase. As they do, Sgt. Grant exits the archway under the aquaduct.)

Sgt. Grant:  Sir, there seems to be no way up from within the tunnel. I’ll have to look elsewhere.

(Sgt. Grant looks over the railing along the staircase. The bottom is not visible.)

Lt. Gibson:  Understood, thank you, Sarge.

(Lt. Gibson enters the overgrown area at the bottom left of the painting. Nothing seems out of the ordinary except the size of the plants. 5 minutes later, Private Jones and Private Williams was back up the staircase.)

Pvt. Jones:  The staircase is a deadend, sir. It ends at a sheer cliff off the side of the building.

Lt. Gibson:  Thank you, Private.

(Sgt. Grant appears on the balcony above the archway.)

Sgt. Grant:  Letting down the rope, sir.

(Sgt. Grant ties off a rope and lowers it off the balcony. The rest of the squad scales the wall using the rope. The aqueduct appears to zig-zag, but each corner is directly above another. It appears to be using the principle that turning 90 degrees moves it backwards, although the way it rises is unknown.)

Lt. Gibson:  Alright, let’s take the measurements and get out of here.

(The team gets to work doing preassigned expirements with the aqueduct.)

 





